    "lem-linear-contrast-statistics-are-borel-measurable": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every linear $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ there is a unique matrix $A=(a_{ij})_{i<n,j<m}$ such that $(Lh)_i=\\sum_{j<m}a_{ij}h_j$. Moreover there is $K\\ge0$ with $\\|Lh\\|_2\\le K\\|h\\|_2$ for every $h$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-metric-regularity-hierarchy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces ([[def-metric-space]]) and let\n$f : X \\to Y$ be a function, with the three regularity conditions as in\n[[def-lipschitz-holder-contraction]]. Then:\n\n1. If $f$ is a contraction, it is Lipschitz.\n2. If $f$ is Lipschitz, it is uniformly continuous\n   ([[def-metric-uniform-continuity]]).\n3. If $f$ is uniformly continuous, it is continuous\n   ([[def-metric-continuity]]).\n4. If $f$ is $\\alpha$-Hölder for some rational $\\alpha$ with $0 < \\alpha \\le 1$,\n   it is uniformly continuous.\n5. Suppose $X$ is nonempty and bounded, and put\n   $R := \\operatorname{diam}(X) + 1$, a real with $R > 0$\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]). If $f$ is Lipschitz with constant $L$, then\n   for **every** rational $\\alpha$ with $0 < \\alpha \\le 1$ the map $f$ is\n   $\\alpha$-Hölder with constant $L\\,R^{\\,1-\\alpha}$.\n\n**Claim 5 carries its boundedness hypothesis for a reason, and no unconditional\n\"Lipschitz implies Hölder\" is asserted anywhere here.** On an unbounded space the\nimplication is false; the witness and its verification are in the first remark\nbelow.\n\n**Strictness is not claimed.** The five implications are asserted and nothing\nmore; that none of them reverses is witnessed elsewhere, and those witnesses are\nnot prerequisites of this theorem. See the second remark.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:X\\to Y$ is continuous between topological spaces, then\n$f^{-1}[B]\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ for every $B\\in\\mathcal B(Y)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$ and $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$ be measurable spaces\n([[def-measurable-space]]). A function $f : X \\to Y$ is **measurable** when\n\n$$f^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{A} \\qquad \\text{for every } B \\in \\mathcal{B}.$$\n\nEquivalently, the family\n\n$$\\mathcal{C}_f := \\{\\, B \\subseteq Y : f^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{A} \\,\\}$$\n\nis a sigma-algebra on $Y$ containing $\\mathcal{B}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-metrics-on-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$. A von Neumann natural is the set of its\npredecessors, $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), so it can be\nused directly as an index set. Define\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^n := \\{\\, x : x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\},$$\n\nand write $x_k$ for $x(k)$, $k < n$. Two elements of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are equal\nexactly when they agree at every $k < n$, functions being equal when they have\nthe same values. For $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ put\n\n$$d_1(x,y) := \\sum_{k<n} |x_k - y_k|, \\qquad d_2(x,y) := \\sqrt{\\ \\sum_{k<n} (x_k - y_k)^2\\ }, \\qquad d_\\infty(x,y) := \\max\\{\\, |x_k - y_k| : k < n \\,\\}.$$\n\nAll three are well defined: the finite sums are those of [[def-finite-sum]]; the\nsum of squares is nonnegative ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[lem-of-square-positive]])\nso it has a unique nonnegative square root ([[thm-of-square-roots]]); and\n$\\{|x_k - y_k| : k < n\\}$ is a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$, because\n$n \\ge 1$, so it has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).\n\n**Then $d_1$, $d_2$ and $d_\\infty$ are metrics on $\\mathbb{R}^n$**\n([[def-metric-space]]).\n\n**Why $n \\ge 1$.** For $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^0$ has exactly one element,\nthe empty function, and $d_1$ and $d_2$ are the empty sum $0$ and its root; but\n$d_\\infty$ would be the maximum of the empty set, which does not exist. The\nhypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is therefore not decoration, and it is carried by every\nstatement about $d_\\infty$ in this library.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-metric-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).\n\nA subset $U \\subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \\in U$ there is a\nreal $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \\subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset\n$F \\subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \\setminus F$ is\nopen.\n\nThe collection\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_d := \\{\\, U \\subseteq X : U \\text{ is open in } (X,d) \\,\\}$$\n\nof all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$\nthat is both open and closed is called **clopen**.\n\n**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\\emptyset$ is open, because the\ndefining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because\n$B(x,r) \\subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and\n$\\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.\n\n**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any set containing an open set that\ncontains $x$; an **open neighbourhood** is an open set containing $x$. In a\nmetric space, a set is a neighbourhood of $x$ exactly when it contains some\nball $B(x,r)$ with $r>0$. Thus $U$ is open exactly when it is a neighbourhood of\neach of its points, and metric balls suffice to test this condition.\n\n**The metric, not the set, determines $\\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same\nset may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the\nsame one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal T)$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). The\n**Borel sigma-algebra of $X$** is the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets:\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X):=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal T).$$\n\nIts members are the **Borel subsets** of $X$. The generated sigma-algebra exists\nand is minimal by [[thm-generated-sigma-algebra-exists-and-is-minimal]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The map $\\ell_a$ is linear. By [F1] and the Euclidean metric convention in [F5], choose $K\\ge0$ such that $|\\ell_a(h)|\\le K\\|h\\|_2$ for every $h\\in\\mathbb R^n$. Thus for $y,z\\in\\mathbb R^n$, $|\\ell_a(y)-\\ell_a(z)|=|\\ell_a(y-z)|\\le K\\|y-z\\|_2,$ so $\\ell_a$ is Lipschitz.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], $\\ell_a$ is continuous for the Euclidean metric topologies from [F5]. By [F3], the preimage of every Borel subset of $\\mathbb R$ is Borel in $\\mathbb R^n$; [F4] therefore makes $\\ell_a$ measurable.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
