    "thm-least-squares-fitted-vector-and-solution-set": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-least-squares-solution-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y):=\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{b\\in\\mathbb R^p}\\|y-Xb\\|_2^2.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-least-squares-solutions-and-normal-equation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional inner product spaces and let $b\\in W$. A vector $x\\in V$ minimises $\\lVert Tx-b\\rVert$ if and only if\n\n$$T^*(Tx-b)=0,$$\n\nequivalently $T^*Tx=T^*b$. Minimisers exist, and if $x_0$ is one minimiser, then the full set of minimisers is $x_0+\\ker T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-matrix-of-the-adjoint-is-the-conjugate-transpose",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional inner product spaces. In orthonormal bases $\\mathcal E=(e_j)$ of $V$ and $\\mathcal F=(f_i)$ of $W$,\n\n$$[T^*]_{\\mathcal E\\leftarrow\\mathcal F}=\\overline{[T]_{\\mathcal F\\leftarrow\\mathcal E}}^{\\mathsf T}.$$\n\nOver $\\mathbb R$ this is the transpose. The statement includes zero-sized bases.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-orthogonal-projection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a subspace of a finite-dimensional inner product space $V$. The orthogonal-decomposition theorem ([[thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition]]) gives\n\n$$V=W\\oplus W^\\perp$$\n\nand associates to every $v\\in V$ unique vectors $w\\in W$ and $z\\in W^\\perp$ with $v=w+z$. The **orthogonal projection onto $W$** is the function\n\n$$P_W:V\\longrightarrow W,\\qquad P_Wv=w.$$\n\nEquivalently, $P_Wv$ is the unique vector of $W$ such that $v-P_Wv\\in W^\\perp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-orthogonal-projection-is-the-unique-nearest-point",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a subspace of a finite-dimensional inner product space $V$. For every $v\\in V$, the vector $P_Wv$ is the unique point of $W$ nearest to $v$: for every $w\\in W$,\n\n$$\\lVert v-P_Wv\\rVert\\le\\lVert v-w\\rVert,$$\n\nwith equality if and only if $w=P_Wv$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-rank-nullity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map of vector spaces over $F$, with $V$\nfinite-dimensional. Then\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\operatorname{nullity}T+\\operatorname{rank}T.$$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\dim_F(\\ker T)+\\dim_F(\\operatorname{im}T).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Regard $X$ as the linear map $T_X:\\mathbb R^p\\to\\mathbb R^n$, $T_Xb=Xb$, with the Euclidean inner products of [F3]. Since $t\\mapsto t^2$ is strictly increasing for $t\\ge0$, minimizing $\\|y-Xb\\|_2^2$ is equivalent to minimizing $\\|Xb-y\\|_2$. Applying [F2] and identifying $T_X^*=X^T$ by [F4] gives a minimizer, the normal equation $X^T(Xb-y)=0$, and the full solution set $b_0+\\ker X$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The image of $T_X$ is $C$. Every minimizer maps to a point of $C$ nearest to $y$, and every point of $C$ has a preimage under $T_X$. By [F5] and [F6] that nearest point is exactly $P_Cy$ and is unique. Thus every minimizing $b$ has fitted vector $Xb=P_Cy$, and its residual is the unique vector $y-P_Cy$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By rank-nullity [F7], $\\dim\\ker X=p-\\dim\\operatorname{im}X=p-\\operatorname{rank}(X)$. Translation preserves dimension, so the affine solution set $b_0+\\ker X$ has the same dimension.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The Statement assumes n,p>=1, so empty domains are excluded."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 gives fit 0 and residual 0 when y=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1-2.1 include n=p=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1-2.1 allow X=0; then the fit is P_{0}y=0 and the solution set is all R^p."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem has no interval or ordered endpoint."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 uses one preimage of P_C y under X; this is a single existential witness and does not use Choice."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 applies the published normal-equation characterization to each minimizer."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 applies both directions of the published normal-equation characterization."
        }
      ]
    },
    "def-linear-estimability-in-fixed-design": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "definition",
      "citations": [],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The model dimensions n and p are positive."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Definition: c=0 is an allowed contrast and a=0 is an allowed statistic."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
