    "thm-linear-estimability-iff-row-space": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-fixed-design-linear-regression-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n,p\\ge1$ be integers and let $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ be a fixed matrix.\nA **fixed-design first-moment linear regression model** is a parametric\nstatistical experiment on $(\\mathbb R^n,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n))$ with parameter\nset $\\mathbb R^p$, laws $(P_\\beta)_{\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^p}$, and observed vector\n$Y(y)=y$, such that every coordinate of $Y$ is integrable under $P_\\beta$ and\n$$\n\\mathbb E_\\beta Y=X\\beta\\qquad(\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^p).\n$$\nHere $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n)$ is the Borel sigma-algebra, and expectation is\ntaken under the particular law $P_\\beta$. The residual vector\n$\\epsilon_\\beta:=Y-X\\beta$ therefore has coordinatewise mean zero. The phrase\n**fixed design** means that $X$ is held constant in this experiment; it carries\nno probability law or assignment mechanism for $X$.\n\nThis definition states only the first-moment model. It imposes no covariance,\nindependence, or Gaussian assumption. Such assumptions must be stated where\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-linear-estimability-in-fixed-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "In a fixed-design first-moment regression model with parameter\n$\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^p$, a **linear contrast** is the estimand\n$$\n\\psi_c(\\beta):=c^T\\beta,\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb R^p.\n$$\nThe contrast is **linearly estimable** if there is a fixed vector\n$a\\in\\mathbb R^n$ such that the statistic $a^TY$ is unbiased for $\\psi_c$ at\nevery parameter value:\n$$\n\\mathbb E_\\beta[a^TY]=c^T\\beta\\qquad\\text{for every }\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^p.\n$$\nThe vector $a$ may depend on the fixed design and on $c$, but not on the\nobserved response or on the unknown parameter. The map $y\\mapsto a^Ty$ is a\nmeasurable statistic by\n[[lem-linear-contrast-statistics-are-borel-measurable]] and\n[[def-statistic-and-estimator]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For any fixed $a$, each coordinate of $Y$ is integrable by [F1], so the finite linear combination $a^TY$ is integrable. Linearity [F3] gives $\\mathbb E_\\beta[a^TY]=a^T\\mathbb E_\\beta Y=a^TX\\beta=(X^Ta)^T\\beta.$",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $X^Ta=c$, step 1.1 gives $\\mathbb E_\\beta[a^TY]=c^T\\beta$ for every $\\beta$. By [F2] the contrast is linearly estimable. The equation $X^Ta=c$ says exactly that $c\\in\\operatorname{im}(X^T)=\\operatorname{row}(X)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Conversely, suppose $c^T\\beta$ is linearly estimable. By [F2] there is a fixed $a$ for which the expectation in step 1.1 equals $c^T\\beta$ for every $\\beta$. Thus $(X^Ta-c)^T\\beta=0$ for every $\\beta$; taking $\\beta$ equal to each standard basis vector of $\\mathbb R^p$ shows coordinatewise that $X^Ta=c$. Hence $c$ belongs to the row space.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Steps 2.1 and 2.2 prove both implications, and step 1.1 proves the stated unbiasedness for every representative $a$ with $X^Ta=c$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The Statement uses positive finite dimensions n and p."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 covers c=0 with a=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 2.1 and 2.1 apply when n=p=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For X=0, the Statement reduces to c=0 iff X^T a=0; steps 2.1 and 2.1 remain valid."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or ordered parameter occurs."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.2 uses one estimator witness a supplied by the definition; no family is chosen."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 proves X^T a=c implies estimability."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.2 proves estimability implies X^T a=c."
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-ols-unbiasedness-and-covariance-full-rank": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-fixed-design-linear-regression-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n,p\\ge1$ be integers and let $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ be a fixed matrix.\nA **fixed-design first-moment linear regression model** is a parametric\nstatistical experiment on $(\\mathbb R^n,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n))$ with parameter\nset $\\mathbb R^p$, laws $(P_\\beta)_{\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^p}$, and observed vector\n$Y(y)=y$, such that every coordinate of $Y$ is integrable under $P_\\beta$ and\n$$\n\\mathbb E_\\beta Y=X\\beta\\qquad(\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^p).\n$$\nHere $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n)$ is the Borel sigma-algebra, and expectation is\ntaken under the particular law $P_\\beta$. The residual vector\n$\\epsilon_\\beta:=Y-X\\beta$ therefore has coordinatewise mean zero. The phrase\n**fixed design** means that $X$ is held constant in this experiment; it carries\nno probability law or assignment mechanism for $X$.\n\nThis definition states only the first-moment model. It imposes no covariance,\nindependence, or Gaussian assumption. Such assumptions must be stated where\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-least-squares-fitted-vector-and-solution-set",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n,p\\ge1$, $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$, $y\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and\n$C=\\operatorname{col}(X)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$. For the least-squares solution\nset $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$ of\n[[def-least-squares-solution-set]], the following hold:\n\n1. $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$ is nonempty, and $b$ is in it exactly when\n   $X^T(Xb-y)=0$.\n2. If $b_0\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$, then\n   $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)=b_0+\\ker X$.\n3. Every least-squares solution has the same fitted vector $P_Cy$ and residual\n   $y-P_Cy$.\n4. The affine solution set has dimension $p-\\operatorname{rank}(X)$.\n\nThe theorem makes no statistical moment or distributional assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rank-nullity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map of vector spaces over $F$, with $V$\nfinite-dimensional. Then\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\operatorname{nullity}T+\\operatorname{rank}T.$$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\\dim_F V=\\dim_F(\\ker T)+\\dim_F(\\operatorname{im}T).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Y_j)_{j\\in J}$ be finite families of square-integrable real random variables on one common probability space. Then covariance is symmetric, and for real scalars $a_i,b_j$,\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i,\\sum_{j\\in J}b_jY_j\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{j\\in J}a_ib_j\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).$$\nThis includes empty sums and constant summands. On a finite probability space all real random variables are square-integrable, so the original finite-space case is included with the same centered-product covariance.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Full column rank means $\\ker X=\\{0\\}$. By [F3], $\\operatorname{rank}(X)=p$. For every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^p$, $v^TX^TXv=\\|Xv\\|_2^2>0,$ so $X^TX$ is injective and hence invertible by rank-nullity. The normal equation in [F2] now gives the unique minimizer $\\widehat\\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Write $A=(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$. By the fixed-design model [F1], $\\epsilon_\\beta=Y-X\\beta$, so $Y=X\\beta+\\epsilon_\\beta$. Step 1.1 gives $\\widehat\\beta=\\beta+A\\epsilon_\\beta$. By [F4] and [A1], $\\mathbb E_\\beta\\widehat\\beta=\\beta+A\\mathbb E_\\beta\\epsilon_\\beta=\\beta.$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F5] and [F6], the $(r,s)$ entry of the covariance matrix of $A\\epsilon_\\beta$ is $\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ri}A_{sj}\\operatorname{Cov}_\\beta(\\epsilon_{\\beta,i},\\epsilon_{\\beta,j})=\\sigma^2\\sum_{i=1}^n A_{ri}A_{si}.$ Thus $\\operatorname{Cov}_\\beta(\\widehat\\beta)=\\sigma^2AA^T$ by step 2.1. Since $X^TX$ is symmetric, $AA^T=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TX(X^TX)^{-1}=(X^TX)^{-1}.$ No step uses the shape of the error distribution beyond its stated moments.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The Statement assumes n,p>=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: beta=0 is allowed, while sigma^2>0 excludes zero covariance; step 2.1 proves the mean formula for beta=0 as well."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 covers n=p=1 whenever the one design column is nonzero."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Rank-deficient X and sigma^2=0 are explicitly excluded by full rank and sigma^2>0."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or ordered parameter occurs."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The full-rank least-squares coefficient is unique, so no selection is made."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem states formulas, not an equivalence."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem states formulas, not an equivalence."
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-gauss-markov-for-fixed-design-regression": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-ols-unbiasedness-and-covariance-full-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ have full column rank in a fixed-design\nfirst-moment regression model. Suppose for some $\\sigma^2>0$ the error vector\n$\\epsilon_\\beta:=Y-X\\beta$ has finite second moments,\n$\\mathbb E_\\beta\\epsilon_\\beta=0$, and\n$\\operatorname{Cov}_\\beta(\\epsilon_\\beta)=\\sigma^2 I_n$ for every $\\beta$,\nwhere the vector covariance matrix has entries\n$\\operatorname{Cov}_\\beta(\\epsilon_{\\beta,i},\\epsilon_{\\beta,j})$.\nThen the unique least-squares estimator is\n$$\n\\widehat\\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY,\n$$\nit is unbiased for $\\beta$, and its covariance matrix is\n$$\n\\operatorname{Cov}_\\beta(\\widehat\\beta)=\\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}.\n$$\nNo Gaussian assumption is made.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-linear-estimability-iff-row-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a fixed-design first-moment regression model with $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$,\na contrast $c^T\\beta$ is linearly estimable if and only if\n$$\nc\\in\\operatorname{row}(X)=\\operatorname{im}(X^T),\n$$\nequivalently if and only if there exists $a\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $X^Ta=c$.\nWhenever this holds, $a^TY$ is unbiased for $c^T\\beta$ for every $\\beta$.\nHere $\\operatorname{row}(X)$ is defined as the image of the transpose map.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Y_j)_{j\\in J}$ be finite families of square-integrable real random variables on one common probability space. Then covariance is symmetric, and for real scalars $a_i,b_j$,\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i,\\sum_{j\\in J}b_jY_j\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{j\\in J}a_ib_j\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).$$\nThis includes empty sums and constant summands. On a finite probability space all real random variables are square-integrable, so the original finite-space case is included with the same centered-product covariance.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Define $a_0:=X(X^TX)^{-1}c\\in\\mathbb R^n$. Then $X^Ta_0=c$ and $a_0^TY=c^T(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY=c^T\\widehat\\beta$. By [F2], $c^T\\beta$ is estimable and $a_0^TY$ is unbiased.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $a^TY$ be any linear unbiased estimator of the same contrast. By [F2], $X^Ta=c$. Set $q:=a-a_0$. Then $X^Tq=0$, while $a_0\\in\\operatorname{col}(X)$; hence $q^Ta_0=0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "From $Y=X\\beta+\\epsilon_\\beta$, [A1], [F3], and [F4], $\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(a^TY)=\\sigma^2\\|a\\|_2^2=\\sigma^2\\|a_0+q\\|_2^2=\\sigma^2\\|a_0\\|_2^2+\\sigma^2\\|q\\|_2^2\\ge \\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(a_0^TY).$ Since $\\sigma^2>0$, equality holds exactly when $q=0$. Therefore the OLS contrast estimator has minimum variance among all linear unbiased estimates.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The Statement assumes positive dimensions and a full-column-rank design."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 3.1 covers c=0: the OLS contrast has variance zero and every competing variance is nonnegative."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1-2.1 include n=p=1 under the stated positive variance assumption."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Rank-deficient designs and sigma^2=0 are outside the stated hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or ordered parameter occurs."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The proof constructs one coefficient vector a_0 and handles an arbitrary competitor; no family is selected."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem states an optimality claim, not an equivalence."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem states an optimality claim, not an equivalence."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-collinear-fixed-design-estimability": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "example",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-least-squares-fitted-vector-and-solution-set",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n,p\\ge1$, $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$, $y\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and\n$C=\\operatorname{col}(X)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$. For the least-squares solution\nset $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$ of\n[[def-least-squares-solution-set]], the following hold:\n\n1. $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$ is nonempty, and $b$ is in it exactly when\n   $X^T(Xb-y)=0$.\n2. If $b_0\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$, then\n   $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)=b_0+\\ker X$.\n3. Every least-squares solution has the same fitted vector $P_Cy$ and residual\n   $y-P_Cy$.\n4. The affine solution set has dimension $p-\\operatorname{rank}(X)$.\n\nThe theorem makes no statistical moment or distributional assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-linear-estimability-iff-row-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a fixed-design first-moment regression model with $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$,\na contrast $c^T\\beta$ is linearly estimable if and only if\n$$\nc\\in\\operatorname{row}(X)=\\operatorname{im}(X^T),\n$$\nequivalently if and only if there exists $a\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $X^Ta=c$.\nWhenever this holds, $a^TY$ is unbiased for $c^T\\beta$ for every $\\beta$.\nHere $\\operatorname{row}(X)$ is defined as the image of the transpose map.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $b=(b_1,b_2)^T$, put $s=b_1+b_2$. Then $Xb=(s,s)^T$, and the squared residual is $(1-s)^2+(3-s)^2=2(s-2)^2+2.$ It is minimized exactly when $s=2$. Thus the full coefficient solution set is $(1,1)^T+t(1,-1)^T$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$, its fitted vector is $(2,2)^T$, and its residual is $(-1,1)^T$, in agreement with [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The row space of $X$ is $\\operatorname{span}\\{(1,1)^T\\}$. Hence $c=(1,1)^T$ is in the row space and is estimable; indeed $X^T(1/2,1/2)^T=(1,1)^T$, so $(Y_1+Y_2)/2$ estimates $\\beta_1+\\beta_2$ unbiasedly. The vector $(1,0)^T$ is not in that row space, since every vector $X^Ta$ has equal coordinates. Therefore neither single coefficient is linearly estimable by [F2].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
