    "cex-heteroskedasticity-invalidates-ols-blue": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "counterexample",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-gauss-markov-for-fixed-design-regression",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ have full column rank in a fixed-design\nfirst-moment regression model. Suppose\n$\\operatorname{Cov}_\\beta(\\epsilon_\\beta)=\\sigma^2 I_n$ for a fixed\n$\\sigma^2>0$, where the vector covariance matrix has entries\n$\\operatorname{Cov}_\\beta(\\epsilon_{\\beta,i},\\epsilon_{\\beta,j})$. For every\ncontrast $c^T\\beta$, the OLS estimate\n$c^T\\widehat\\beta$ is unbiased and has variance no larger than any other\nlinear unbiased estimator of that contrast. Thus OLS is **best linear\nunbiased (BLUE)** for every estimable contrast. Here “best” means minimum\nvariance within the class of linear unbiased estimators; Gaussianity is not\nassumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Y_j)_{j\\in J}$ be finite families of square-integrable real random variables on one common probability space. Then covariance is symmetric, and for real scalars $a_i,b_j$,\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i,\\sum_{j\\in J}b_jY_j\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{j\\in J}a_ib_j\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).$$\nThis includes empty sums and constant summands. On a finite probability space all real random variables are square-integrable, so the original finite-space case is included with the same centered-product covariance.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-expectation-agrees-with-the-finite-weighted-sum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,w)$ be a finite probability space and let $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$.\nAfter identifying $(\\Omega,w)$ with the full-power-set probability space of\n[[thm-finite-probability-spaces-are-exactly-finite-full-power-set-probability-spaces]],\nthe expectation defined by\n[[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]] agrees with\nthe published finite formulas:\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega)=\\sum_{x\\in X(\\Omega)}x\\,\\mathbb P(X=x).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The four point masses sum to $4(1/4)=1$, so they define a probability measure by [F4]. Directly summing over the four equally weighted outcomes using [F3] gives $\\mathbb E U=\\mathbb E V=0$, $\\mathbb E U^2=\\mathbb E V^2=1$, and $\\mathbb E(UV)=0$. Thus $\\mathbb E\\epsilon=0$ and, by [F5], $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\epsilon)=\\operatorname{diag}(1,9)$, which is not a scalar multiple of $I_2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Here $X^TX=2$, so the unique OLS estimate is $\\widehat\\beta=\\frac{Y_1+Y_2}{2}=\\beta+\\frac{U+3V}{2}.$ It is unbiased by [F2], and bilinearity gives $\\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat\\beta)=(1+9)/4=5/2$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The alternative estimator $\\widetilde\\beta=\\frac9{10}Y_1+\\frac1{10}Y_2=\\beta+\\frac9{10}U+\\frac3{10}V$ is linear and unbiased because its coefficients sum to one and both error means vanish. Its variance is $\\frac{81}{100}\\operatorname{Var}(U)+\\frac9{100}\\operatorname{Var}(V)+\\frac{54}{100}\\operatorname{Cov}(U,V)=\\frac9{10}<\\frac52.$ So OLS is not BLUE in this model. The covariance premise in [F1] fails, which is the stated boundary of the Gauss–Markov theorem.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The counterexample has two observations and a nonempty four-point sample space."
        },
