    "ex-high-leverage-is-a-design-feature": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "example",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-least-squares-fitted-vector-and-solution-set",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n,p\\ge1$, $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$, $y\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and\n$C=\\operatorname{col}(X)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$. For the least-squares solution\nset $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$ of\n[[def-least-squares-solution-set]], the following hold:\n\n1. $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$ is nonempty, and $b$ is in it exactly when\n   $X^T(Xb-y)=0$.\n2. If $b_0\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)$, then\n   $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{LS}}(y)=b_0+\\ker X$.\n3. Every least-squares solution has the same fitted vector $P_Cy$ and residual\n   $y-P_Cy$.\n4. The affine solution set has dimension $p-\\operatorname{rank}(X)$.\n\nThe theorem makes no statistical moment or distributional assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-orthogonal-projection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a subspace of a finite-dimensional inner product space $V$. The orthogonal-decomposition theorem ([[thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition]]) gives\n\n$$V=W\\oplus W^\\perp$$\n\nand associates to every $v\\in V$ unique vectors $w\\in W$ and $z\\in W^\\perp$ with $v=w+z$. The **orthogonal projection onto $W$** is the function\n\n$$P_W:V\\longrightarrow W,\\qquad P_Wv=w.$$\n\nEquivalently, $P_Wv$ is the unique vector of $W$ such that $v-P_Wv\\in W^\\perp$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The predictor mean is $\\bar x=26/5$. Put $z=x-\\bar x\\mathbf1=(-26,-21,-16,-11,74)^T/5$. Then $\\mathbf1^Tz=0$ and $z^Tz=\\frac{676+441+256+121+5476}{25}=\\frac{1394}{5}.$ Since $x=z+\\bar x\\mathbf1$, the column space is $C=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\mathbf1,z\\}$, with these two generators nonzero and orthogonal.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For every $y\\in\\mathbb R^5$, the vector $p(y)=\\frac{\\mathbf1^Ty}{5}\\mathbf1+\\frac{z^Ty}{z^Tz}z$ lies in $C$, and $y-p(y)$ is orthogonal to both generators, hence to all of $C$. By [F2], $p(y)=P_Cy$; [F1] identifies this as the fitted vector. Therefore $H=\\frac{\\mathbf1\\mathbf1^T}{5}+\\frac{zz^T}{z^Tz},\\qquad h_{55}=\\frac15+\\frac{(74/5)^2}{1394/5}=\\frac15+\\frac{2738}{3485}=\\frac{687}{697}.$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Since $h_{55}=1-10/697$ is close to one and its formula involves only the design vector, this is a high-leverage design row. The calculation makes no claim that the fifth response has a large residual or is influential; those are separate diagnostics.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "boundaries": [
